### What is the service registry?

A service registry is a database of currently running instances keyed by service name. Each instance, when it starts, registers itself with its host, port, and metadata, then sends periodic heartbeats to prove it is alive. Netflix Eureka, HashiCorp Consul, and etcd are common registries.

```java
package com.bank.payment;

import org.springframework.boot.SpringApplication;
import org.springframework.boot.autoconfigure.SpringBootApplication;
import org.springframework.cloud.client.discovery.EnableDiscoveryClient;

@SpringBootApplication
@EnableDiscoveryClient
public class PaymentServiceApplication {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        SpringApplication.run(PaymentServiceApplication.class, args);
        System.out.println("Payment service registered with Eureka as payment-service");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Payment service registered with Eureka as payment-service
```

### How does client-side discovery work?

In client-side discovery the caller fetches the full list of healthy instances from the registry and picks one itself using a load-balancing rule like round-robin. Spring Cloud LoadBalancer does exactly this when the caller uses the `lb://` URI scheme.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import org.springframework.web.reactive.function.client.WebClient;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

@Service
public class TransferService {

    private final WebClient.Builder webClientBuilder;

    public TransferService(WebClient.Builder webClientBuilder) {
        this.webClientBuilder = webClientBuilder;
    }

    public String initiateTransfer(String fromAccount, String toAccount, BigDecimal amount) {
        // lb:// triggers client-side discovery and load balancing
        return webClientBuilder.build()
                .post()
                .uri("lb://payment-service/api/v1/transfers")
                .bodyValue(new TransferRequest(fromAccount, toAccount, amount))
                .retrieve()
                .bodyToMono(String.class)
                .block();
    }

    record TransferRequest(String fromAccount, String toAccount, BigDecimal amount) {}
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Transfer TXN-20240115-001 initiated from SAV2001 to SAV2002
```

### How does server-side discovery work?

In server-side discovery the caller calls a stable virtual address, such as a Kubernetes Service or a cloud load balancer, and that layer consults the registry and forwards the request. The caller carries no discovery logic at all.

```yaml
# Kubernetes Service definition for payment-service
apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: payment-service
  namespace: banking-prod
spec:
  selector:
    app: payment-service
  ports:
    - port: 8080
      targetPort: 8080
  type: ClusterIP
```

```java
package com.bank.service;

import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import org.springframework.web.reactive.function.client.WebClient;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

@Service
public class TransferServiceK8s {

    // In Kubernetes, the DNS name resolves to the Service's ClusterIP
    private static final String PAYMENT_URL = "http://payment-service.banking-prod.svc.cluster.local:8080";

    private final WebClient webClient;

    public TransferServiceK8s(WebClient.Builder builder) {
        this.webClient = builder.baseUrl(PAYMENT_URL).build();
    }

    public String settlePayment(String accountId, BigDecimal amount) {
        return webClient.post()
                .uri("/api/v1/settle")
                .bodyValue(new SettleRequest(accountId, amount))
                .retrieve()
                .bodyToMono(String.class)
                .block();
    }

    record SettleRequest(String accountId, BigDecimal amount) {}
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Settlement confirmed for SAV2001, amount: 50000.00
```

### How do health checks and load balancing work together?

A liveness check answers whether the process is alive. A readiness check answers whether it can accept traffic right now. A service that is alive but still warming its connection pool should report not-ready so no requests are routed to it.

```java
package com.bank.health;

import org.springframework.boot.actuate.health.Health;
import org.springframework.boot.actuate.health.HealthIndicator;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Component;
import javax.sql.DataSource;
import java.sql.Connection;

@Component
public class CoreBankingHealthIndicator implements HealthIndicator {

    private final DataSource dataSource;

    public CoreBankingHealthIndicator(DataSource dataSource) {
        this.dataSource = dataSource;
    }

    @Override
    public Health health() {
        try (Connection conn = dataSource.getConnection()) {
            if (conn.isValid(2)) {
                return Health.up()
                        .withDetail("database", "NBI core banking DB reachable")
                        .build();
            }
        } catch (Exception e) {
            // Fall through to down
        }
        return Health.down().withDetail("database", "NBI core banking DB unreachable").build();
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output (healthy):
{"status":"UP","details":{"database":"NBI core banking DB reachable"}}
```

### How does a service mesh handle discovery?

A service mesh like Istio or Linkerd injects a sidecar proxy next to each Pod. The sidecar handles discovery, load balancing, retries, and mutual TLS transparently, so the application code stays unaware. The mesh control plane maintains a registry of endpoints and pushes configuration to every sidecar.

```yaml
# Istio VirtualService routing for payment-service
apiVersion: networking.istio.io/v1beta1
kind: VirtualService
metadata:
  name: payment-service
spec:
  hosts:
    - payment-service
  http:
    - route:
        - destination:
            host: payment-service
            port:
              number: 8080
      retries:
        attempts: 3
        perTryTimeout: 2s
```

```text
Istio sidecar intercepts call to payment-service
  -> resolves healthy endpoints from control plane
  -> load-balances to 10.0.3.14:8080
  -> encrypts with mTLS automatically
  -> retries on failure up to 3 times
```

### What are the security concerns?

The registry is a trust anchor. If an attacker registers a fake instance (registry poisoning), they can hijack traffic meant for the real service. Registration must be authenticated and the registry access-controlled. Zero Trust with mutual TLS ensures both caller and callee prove identity with certificates.

```java
package com.bank.security;

// WRONG: No authentication on registration
// Any pod can register as "payment-service" and steal traffic
// eurekaClient.register("payment-service", "10.0.99.1:8080");

// CORRECT: mTLS ensures only pods with valid certificates can register
// and downstream calls verify the callee's certificate
// Istio PeerAuthentication enforces mTLS cluster-wide:
```

```yaml
# Istio PeerAuthentication: enforce mTLS for all services
apiVersion: security.istio.io/v1beta1
kind: PeerAuthentication
metadata:
  name: default
  namespace: banking-prod
spec:
  mtls:
    mode: STRICT
```

```text
With STRICT mTLS:
  - Only services with valid mesh certificates can register
  - Every call is encrypted and both sides verified
  - Registry poisoning is blocked at the transport layer
```

### What about performance and DNS TTL?

DNS-based discovery is subject to TTL caching. A short TTL means faster failover but more DNS lookups. Proxy hops in a mesh add small per-call latency but buy consistent policy. The registry itself must scale under thousands of instances.

```java
package com.bank.config;

import java.net.InetAddress;
import java.security.Security;

public class DnsTtlConfig {
    public static void configureDnsTtl() {
        // JVM caches DNS forever by default with a security manager.
        // Set a short TTL so failover is fast.
        Security.setProperty("networkaddress.cache.ttl", "10");
        Security.setProperty("networkaddress.cache.negative.ttl", "5");
        System.out.println("DNS TTL set to 10 seconds for fast failover");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
DNS TTL set to 10 seconds for fast failover
```

### How do multi-region setups work?

Multi-region setups run a registry per region with federation. Services prefer same-region instances to keep latency low and fail over cross-region only when a whole region's instances are unavailable. Eureka supports zone-aware routing where instances in the same zone are preferred.

```yaml
# Eureka zone-aware configuration
eureka:
  instance:
    metadata-map:
      zone: ap-south-1a
  client:
    prefer-same-zone-eureka: true
    service-url:
      ap-south-1a: http://eureka-mumbai:8761/eureka/
      ap-south-1b: http://eureka-mumbai-b:8761/eureka/
      eu-west-1a: http://eureka-london:8761/eureka/
```

```text
Routing preference:
  1. Same zone (ap-south-1a) -> lowest latency
  2. Same region, different zone (ap-south-1b) -> moderate latency
  3. Cross-region (eu-west-1a) -> last resort, high latency
```
